=== step 6 · block 0 "The Demand for Labour" · quiz index 3
DIAGRAM title: The Demand for Labour
description: IAL 3.3.4 · 1a: one workshop's demand for labour is the value of what each extra machinist adds (extra output × the price of the product, a price the workshop cannot change). A change in the wage moves along it; a change in the product price or in productivity shifts it.
checklist: ["The wage on the vertical axis, the number of workers on the horizontal","A downward-sloping demand curve: each extra worker adds less (diminishing marginal productivity)","The firm hires where the wage line meets the curve","A wage change as a movement along; a price or productivity change as a new curve"]
view: {"label":"The hiring rule"}
  svg text: One workshop: hire while output is worth the wage | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists at one workshop | MRP = demand | W $12 | 6 | At $12 it hires 6: the 6th adds $12. | The next would add $9, less than the wage.
view: {"label":"A higher wage"}
  svg text: A higher wage: a movement along | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists at one workshop | MRP = demand | $12 | $15 | 6 | 5 | Wage $12 → $15: 6 → 5 machinists. | Same curve: only the wage changed.
view: {"label":"Price or productivity rises"}
  svg text: Price or productivity rises: a shift | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists at one workshop | MRP | MRP₁ | W $12 | 6 | 7 | Shirt price $3 → $4: 6 → 7 at $12. | One more shirt each an hour does the same.
QUIZ labour-markets:quiz:9960c68b: A rise in the price of the product a firm sells is most likely to:
  * A. shift its demand for labour to the right
    B. cause a movement along its demand for labour
    C. shift the supply of labour to the right
    D. shift its demand for labour to the left

=== step 12 · block 1 "The Supply of Labour" · quiz index 8
DIAGRAM title: The Supply of Labour
description: IAL 3.3.4 · 2c and 2d: the supply of labour to one occupation, shifted right by net migration and left by a licence requirement, and the same rise in demand met by an elastic and an inelastic supply.
checklist: ["An upward-sloping supply curve, the wage on the vertical axis","Net migration: S shifts right, 12,000 more willing at every wage","A licence, higher income tax or higher benefits: S shifts left","With inelastic supply, a rise in demand raises the wage more and employment less"]
view: {"label":"Net migration"}
  svg text: A net inflow of 12,000 machinists | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists (thousands) | S | S₁ | 12 | 36 | 48 | At $12: 48,000 willing instead of 36,000. | A new curve: more willing at every wage.
view: {"label":"A licence requirement"}
  svg text: A licence some cannot meet | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists (thousands) | S | S₁ | 12 | 36 | 24 | At $12: 24,000 willing instead of 36,000. | Tax or benefit rises shift S left in the same way.
view: {"label":"Elastic and inelastic supply"}
  svg text: The same rise in demand, two supply curves | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists (thousands) | D | D₁ | elastic S | inelastic S | 12 | 36 | 14 | 44 | 16 | 40 | Elastic: $14, 44,000. Inelastic: $16, 40,000. | Inelastic supply: more of the rise goes on pay.
QUIZ labour-markets:quiz:7a9180ea: Which of these would shift the supply of labour to an occupation to the left?
  * A. A new licence the job now requires
    B. A rise in the wage rate for the job
    C. A net inflow of workers from abroad
    D. A cut in income tax rates

=== step 18 · block 2 "Wage Determination in a Competitive Market" · quiz index 14
DIAGRAM title: Labour Market Equilibrium
description: IAL 3.3.4 · 3a and 3b: the machinists' market clears where demand meets supply; one firm takes that wage as a horizontal supply; a shift of demand moves the wage and employment together, a shift of supply moves them apart.
checklist: ["D and S labelled, with the equilibrium wage and quantity marked on both axes","One firm: a horizontal supply at the market wage, hiring where it meets the firm's demand","Demand rises: $14 and 44,000 — both up","Supply rises: $10 and 40,000 — wage down, employment up"]
view: {"label":"Equilibrium"}
  svg text: Labour market equilibrium | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists (thousands) | D | S | 12 | 36 | Wanted = willing at $12: 36,000 machinists. | Below it, a shortage; above it, a surplus.
view: {"label":"One firm: a wage-taker"}
  svg text: One firm: a wage-taker | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists at one workshop | firm's demand | S = W | 12 | 6 | The market sets $12; the firm faces a flat supply there. | It hires 6, where its demand meets that line.
view: {"label":"Demand rises"}
  svg text: Export orders rise: demand shifts right | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists (thousands) | D | S | D₁ | 12 | 36 | 14 | 44 | New equilibrium: $14, 44,000 machinists. | Wage and employment both rise.
view: {"label":"Supply rises"}
  svg text: Net migration: supply shifts right | Wage ($ an hour) | Machinists (thousands) | D | S | S₁ | 12 | 36 | 10 | 40 | New equilibrium: $10, 40,000 machinists. | The wage falls; employment rises.
QUIZ labour-markets:quiz:1896f9c5: At a wage above equilibrium in a competitive labour market there will be:
    A. more workers wanted than are willing
    B. equal numbers wanted and willing
  * C. more workers willing than firms want
    D. a rightward shift of labour demand

